WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.871
- You've been sometimes
labeled, 'a bad vegan.'

00:00:03.871 --> 00:00:05.520
(Danielle laughing)
What does this mean?

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.350
This is Dr. Danielle
Belardo, she's a vegan

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.630
and a cardiologist, but oddly enough

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.110
she's gotten a lot of flack
from the vegan community.

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.540
Let's find out why.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.240
Why is this happening considering

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:19.370
that you're an expert in this
field, as well as a vegan?

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:21.600
- I'm very honest and
transparent about the data

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.360
which is, the truth is, is you don't have

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:28.360
to go 100% vegan to have
health benefits by any means.

00:00:28.670 --> 00:00:30.710
Our American College of
Cardiology guidelines

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.980
I think really are the most robust

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:34.460
and best data in this space.

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:37.260
They essentially say
that eating more plants

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.650
is better than less, eating
a plant-predominant diet

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.800
or a plant-based diet are both great.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.080
- I am glad that you aren't
backing down from that message.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:49.010
You are sticking by
evidence-based medicine.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:50.150
- Yeah.
- Saying what you know,

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:52.060
what we don't know because
there's a lot of that,

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:55.230
especially with how really lackluster

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:58.320
the research has been this
far in the nutrition space.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.750
- Right.
- But I think

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:02.230
we sort of hyper-focused
there right at the outset

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:05.510
of being all about health
when it comes to being vegan.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.330
What does it truly mean to be vegan?

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:10.330
'Cause it's more than
just health aspects of it,

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:11.163
am I correct?

00:01:11.163 --> 00:01:16.163
- Totally, so actually veganism
in itself is not a diet.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:17.750
It's more of an ethical decision,

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.890
it's just abstaining from animal products

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.640
and from essentially adding
harm to animals in any way,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.410
practical and possible.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.520
So things like, for example,

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:30.680
medications that have been
tested on animals, vaccines,

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:32.260
these are things I do support.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:33.860
- We've all seen the documentaries

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:36.337
that preach doom and gloom that say,

00:01:36.337 --> 00:01:37.690
"if you eat one egg a day

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:41.510
that's equivalent to some
horrendous habit like smoking."

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:44.000
What's your reaction when
you hear something like that?

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.050
- I think that we could do a
lot more as vegan physicians

00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:48.950
and there's a small growing
amount of us that are

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.980
doing this, but we could
do a lot more in general

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.210
if we kept our messaging
about nutrition science

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.070
really honest, right?

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:57.480
I don't need to convince
people to go vegan

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.740
by cherry-picking data, or
really extrapolating things

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.280
from nutrition science that aren't there.

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:05.530
Nowadays you want to
get your LDL cholesterol

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:08.370
as low as possible for
cardiovascular risk prevention.

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.610
So, primary prevention versus
also secondary prevention.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:12.500
So, how do we do that?

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:15.410
Well, we want to reduce
saturated fat and a lot of that

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.000
is in animal products and
in a whole egg, there is

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:18.940
saturated fats.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:21.920
If you have high cholesterol,
eating multiple eggs a day

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:24.960
adding more saturated fat
into your diet, probably

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:28.650
not beneficial, but
you know, we just wanna

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:30.940
lower the overall saturated fat

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.190
that a patient's eating to
help reduce their cholesterol.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:37.270
- Now the research that we
have from Elevated LDL Scores,

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:39.360
then matching to outcomes,

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.310
meaning patients actually
getting a heart attack

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.810
as a first time, getting
a secondary heart attack,

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.020
even losing their lives.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.340
Do we feel that those end
results are correlated

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:49.900
to the LDL scores

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:53.200
of people who are eating
those saturated food products

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:56.370
and living a very unhealthy
lifestyle with that?

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:57.690
- So, starting with what we know,

00:02:57.690 --> 00:02:59.870
even on these metabolic ward studies.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:02.090
The link between saturated fat

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:06.270
and raising LDL cholesterol
is so well demonstrated.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:08.440
It's been demonstrated
in metabolic ward studies

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:09.273
for years and years.

00:03:09.273 --> 00:03:11.420
Like hundreds of studies have shown us

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.110
that raising your LDL cholesterol

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.940
by eating saturated fat is,

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.990
it's predictable in a linear fashion.

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:18.860
You would also help to
lower your LDL cholesterol

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:19.760
be eating fiber.

00:03:19.760 --> 00:03:22.250
And the only thing that
fiber is in is plants.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:26.947
One of the things that we also
have the most robust research

00:03:26.947 --> 00:03:31.010
in cardiology history
is the predictability

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:36.010
of LDL cholesterol being
causative in atherosclerosis.

00:03:36.793 --> 00:03:39.090
So atherosclerosis is
when essentially you start

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:41.310
to have, your vessels start to harden

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.760
because you have plaque buildup.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.490
And actually, all of us start
to develop atherosclerosis

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:46.960
even when we're young.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.140
It's just how bad it builds
up while you get older.

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.250
When people have other risk factors,

00:03:52.250 --> 00:03:55.040
so things like they're a
smoker, or they have diabetes

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:57.440
or they have high blood
pressure that makes them

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:00.460
actually more at risk for
cardiovascular disease

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.550
because the endothelial lining,

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:05.690
so the lining of the arteries

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:07.790
ends up becoming weaker and more damaged.

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.300
And so that LDL cholesterol
can really get in there

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:11.440
and cause worse plaque.

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:13.170
- I see a difference in that

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.250
if someone can have an LDL of 90,

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.700
versus if they raise
their LDL by introducing

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:23.170
let's say one egg a day or
chicken into their lifestyle

00:04:23.170 --> 00:04:25.070
and now it goes up to 110,

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:28.950
have we seen the difference
between a 90 and a 110

00:04:28.950 --> 00:04:30.960
in the form of primary prevention?

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:34.210
Or is it solely if they
go extreme and introduce

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.760
a lot of saturated fats,

00:04:36.760 --> 00:04:40.070
go full keto that you start
seeing the 190s, 200s figures.

00:04:40.070 --> 00:04:41.460
- For anyone listening that doesn't know,

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.350
understand the difference.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:45.320
So primary prevention is
preventing heart disease

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.180
before you have a cardiac event,

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.320
so a heart attack or something like that.

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:51.960
Secondary prevention is
preventing a heart attack

00:04:51.960 --> 00:04:56.240
when someone has already
had a heart event.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:57.210
So whether it's a stroke

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:59.810
or they already have underlying
cardiovascular disease.

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:02.503
For LDL targets in primary prevention,

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.160
the more and more data that's coming out,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:07.600
we really want your
lifetime exposure of LDL

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.270
to be as low as possible.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.180
So, I would say, yes you want, you're,

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.610
and someone without heart disease

00:05:12.610 --> 00:05:13.830
and without other risk factors,

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:16.240
keep your LDL below 100.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.020
And if it's, the difference
between a 100 or 110 or 90,

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:22.680
it's not quite significant.

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:24.850
And then where it really matters is also

00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:26.490
in secondary prevention.

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.030
You know, now the ESC guidelines

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:30.820
of the European Society of Cardiology,

00:05:30.820 --> 00:05:33.150
they're a little ahead of us with ACC.

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:34.680
Now for secondary prevention,

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:37.710
we want our patients' LDL less than 55.

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:40.560
- The conversation that
happens online the most

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.590
is usually surrounding the
primary prevention aspect

00:05:44.590 --> 00:05:46.490
in that, like I'm a healthy person,

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.600
I eat some protein,
chicken, fish, et cetera.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.260
Am I increasing my risk?

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:52.850
And when I think about this,

00:05:52.850 --> 00:05:55.050
I am not a cardiologist, you are.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:57.830
So, not that I'm saying you
don't think holistically,

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:01.250
to me, I have to balance so
many risks for this patient

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.150
in their everyday life.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:05.290
That I have to sort of pick my battles

00:06:05.290 --> 00:06:08.700
as to which risk is
gonna be the biggest risk

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.020
because I can give someone 10
things to change in their life

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:14.210
and really be honestly expecting

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:15.470
for all these things to happen.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.140
Especially when it comes to food.

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:19.580
Food holds such an
important part in culture,

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.240
in society and in manners.

00:06:22.240 --> 00:06:25.240
So, I have to tread
lightly when I'm giving

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:26.610
this type of advice.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.800
And that's why I'm trying to figure out,

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.750
how much of a battle should I make it

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:33.090
and where should it fall
on the priority scheme.

00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:35.540
- So, one important point you've just made

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:38.790
which is that I emphasize always is that

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.560
we have to meet patients where they're at.

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.890
There is no all or nothing.

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:45.190
Patients don't have to go 100% plant-based

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:49.130
or 100% Mediterranean,
literally small changes count.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:52.280
And the reason why that's
not popular in social media

00:06:52.280 --> 00:06:56.200
is because everyone wants to
sell a diet as a cure-all,

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:57.890
and as the only way,

00:06:57.890 --> 00:06:59.610
'cause they want their diet book to sell,

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:02.370
when in reality, the truth is
somewhere in between, right?

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:06.180
The philosophy I live by is
I just present my patients

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:07.550
with the options in the data

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.910
and then I'm there to support
them in any way I can help.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:11.880
- Do you think there
are any health benefits

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.390
to consuming meat or fish?

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:15.880
- Red meat, I absolutely think
there's no health benefits.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:16.713
We actually have an

00:07:16.713 --> 00:07:18.730
American College of
Cardiology recommendation

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.950
that's officially, official recommendation

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:22.430
to limit or eliminate red meat.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:27.430
Lean meats are actually recommended
with our ACC guidelines.

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:29.930
So, what's my opinion on lean meat?

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:31.740
Do I think that you need them

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.510
or that they add benefit to your health?

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:34.343
Personally no.

00:07:34.343 --> 00:07:36.500
I think that anyone who's interested

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:39.070
in the literature in this,
if you look at Ron Krauss

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:41.810
did a study, he did a
phenomenal study actually.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:43.390
He did a randomized control trial,

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:46.430
that was a crossover study,
and people were randomized

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:50.580
to either red meat, white
meat or plant protein.

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.630
And he actually controlled
for saturated fat.

00:07:53.630 --> 00:07:55.450
And he looked at LDL cholesterol

00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:57.040
and then he actually crossed them over

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.630
to each of the other arms,
and what did he find?

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:01.260
That actually red meat and white meat,

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:02.830
when he controlled for saturated fat

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:06.050
actually raised cholesterol,
the LDL cholesterol equally.

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:08.560
And the only thing that
didn't was the plant protein.

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:09.920
Now fish is a different story.

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:14.920
So we have consistent
epidemiological research

00:08:15.080 --> 00:08:17.120
that shows us that fish is beneficial.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.310
Fatty fish is beneficial
with cardiovascular disease,

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:21.250
some studies showing us it's neutral.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:23.750
So to me, it could go either way.

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:26.010
And the truth is, is that
with all of these foods

00:08:26.010 --> 00:08:28.040
you can eat, you can eat all of them.

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.730
Like you never need to feel

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.460
that you can eat nothing ever again.

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:37.430
It's just trying to modify
your overall dietary pattern

00:08:37.430 --> 00:08:38.660
to a helpful one.

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:41.070
- Would you say that we have complete data

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:44.520
to say that elimination
of all animal products

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:48.180
is undoubtedly safe and ideal for all.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.400
- I'll start with safe.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.333
Is eliminating 100% animal
products safe for all?

00:08:52.333 --> 00:08:53.700
a 100%, yes.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.260
So, there is absolutely,

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.880
we need absolutely nothing from animals,

00:08:57.880 --> 00:09:01.350
despite what people will tell
you, we can supplement B12.

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.480
The American Academy of
Nutrition and Dietetics

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.160
even has an official formal statement,

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:07.140
that it's actually healthy to be vegan.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.460
Well, it's gotta be a
well-planned plant-based diet,

00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:12.330
is helpful at all stages of life.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.840
We're talking infancy through adulthood.

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:16.930
We have randomized control trial data

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:18.780
showing that plant-based diets are helpful

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:21.640
but also, we also have
epidemiological data.

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.286
Even if you look at the
Seven-day Adventists, right.

00:09:24.286 --> 00:09:27.855
They are some of the longest
living people in the world,

00:09:27.855 --> 00:09:29.370
if you look at the Blue Zones

00:09:29.370 --> 00:09:32.860
and they eat a 100% plant-based diet.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:34.930
Now, your second question
was, is that ideal?

00:09:34.930 --> 00:09:37.970
And this is where I get some
people give me a hard time.

00:09:37.970 --> 00:09:40.650
You do not have to go
on 100% plant-based diet

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.340
to be in ideal health and be healthful.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:45.070
We don't have data that shows that

00:09:45.070 --> 00:09:46.910
eating 100% plant-based diet

00:09:46.910 --> 00:09:49.110
is better than eating
a really plant-centered

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:50.410
or plant-predominant diet

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:52.067
with some amount of animal products.

00:09:52.067 --> 00:09:54.800
- There are certain Blue Zones
that do eat animal products,

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:56.153
certain ones that don't.
- Exactly.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:00.220
- It's important to know that
individuals cross-culturally

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:02.620
have developed different abilities

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:04.130
to extract energies from food,

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:06.150
different reactions to certain foods.

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:08.110
Their gut microbiome is totally different

00:10:08.110 --> 00:10:11.210
which is a whole new thing
that we're exploring.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:13.430
For me I like to be introspective

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:16.540
and think about how we
need to be very humble

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.480
when it comes to even looking
at evidence and medicine.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:22.650
And, you know, you've talked
a lot about of great data of,

00:10:22.650 --> 00:10:25.200
outcomes for primary prevention,
secondary prevention,

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.840
increasing LDL scores when
you eat animal products.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:32.350
We've also seen, this is
historically speaking,

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:35.210
very historically speaking humans started

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:36.210
eating animal products

00:10:36.210 --> 00:10:38.750
at some point in our prehistoric nature.

00:10:38.750 --> 00:10:41.670
There has been some data
showing and speculating

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:44.640
perhaps at times that the
reason we've gotten taller,

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.210
the reasons our minds evolved,

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:47.950
because we started eating animal products.

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:52.020
- I do think people extrapolate
way too much from historic

00:10:52.020 --> 00:10:54.530
things in general,
people were really truly

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:57.980
more of hunter, more gathers
than they were hunters

00:10:57.980 --> 00:11:00.490
and eating lots of plants.

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:03.370
Of course, there are certain
populations like the Niue

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:07.520
who eat a high fat diet
and they do fine with it.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:10.093
So, from the evolutionary standpoint,

00:11:11.505 --> 00:11:14.010
I just think it's, I do not think we need

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:16.120
or require animal products to be healthy.

00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:20.100
- Do you feel like there is
a heavy user bias that exists

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:23.890
with, like, you have people
who follow a vegan lifestyle

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:24.840
are generally healthier.

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:26.140
They're less likely to smoke,

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:28.730
less likely to engage in bad habits,

00:11:28.730 --> 00:11:30.870
getting a poor amounts of sleep,

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:32.430
mental health struggles, et cetera,

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:33.780
than compared to someone who is eating

00:11:33.780 --> 00:11:34.930
the American Standard Diet?

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:37.410
There was one study where they compared

00:11:37.410 --> 00:11:40.610
vegetarians to their friends who ate meat,

00:11:40.610 --> 00:11:43.110
but followed similar lifestyle habits.

00:11:43.110 --> 00:11:46.620
And then people who shopped
that similar health food stores

00:11:46.620 --> 00:11:50.020
who ate animal products
and those who didn't.

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:52.960
And they found actually their
end points were quite similar.

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:55.190
Do you feel like there's
something to be made of this?

00:11:55.190 --> 00:11:57.430
- So healthy user bias is a real thing.

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.660
It could be, you look at
the Seven-Day Adventists,

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:02.250
but this is what's interesting,

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:04.120
is that we actually have
data with heart outcomes

00:12:04.120 --> 00:12:05.410
that totally eliminates that.

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:07.410
So, if you actually look
at what I mentioned again,

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:09.070
randomized controlled trial, right?

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:13.230
Leonhard, they decrease
butter, decrease saturated fat,

00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:16.180
decreased red meat,
increased fruits, vegetables,

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:17.890
whole grains, legumes,

00:12:17.890 --> 00:12:22.430
and the experimental
group had more smokers.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:25.240
And they still had
improved cardiac outcomes

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:26.580
just from what they ate,

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:28.720
just from eating more plants
and less animal products.

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:30.260
And it was not a vegan diet.

00:12:30.260 --> 00:12:32.930
It was just a Mediterranean
style plant fully diet.

00:12:32.930 --> 00:12:35.170
Another randomized controlled
trial was PREDIMED.

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:36.930
And if you looked at
the post-talk analysis,

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:40.640
the PREDIMED data, the post,
the vegetarian subgroup

00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:43.430
actually had the most reduction

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:45.100
in cardiovascular mortality as well.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:47.130
- I think there is such an
important distinction to be made

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:50.440
between population evidence
and an individual guidance.

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.740
- Yes.
- Because it just,

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:55.370
it's incredible how many patients I have

00:12:55.370 --> 00:12:56.720
come through my door,

00:12:56.720 --> 00:13:00.240
that if I even give the
best knowledge of all these

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:02.470
references that you've mentioned,

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:05.470
to think that they're
gonna adapt it right away

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:06.710
is not realistic.

00:13:06.710 --> 00:13:09.860
So, when I don't, I can
see how the vegan community

00:13:09.860 --> 00:13:11.490
can come after me and say,

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:13.250
in one of my videos, I recommended,

00:13:13.250 --> 00:13:15.650
I said like, we should
eliminate as much as we can,

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:16.483
processed foods.

00:13:16.483 --> 00:13:18.150
- I totally agree.
- Including, you know,

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:19.540
like hotdogs out of our diet.

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:21.275
- Yeah, I totally agree.
- But if you go to a ballgame

00:13:21.275 --> 00:13:22.675
and you eat a hot dog to celebrate that

00:13:22.675 --> 00:13:23.947
you're at a ballgame.

00:13:23.947 --> 00:13:25.567
- I totally agree, I totally agree.

00:13:25.567 --> 00:13:27.207
- But I got destroyed on that, like...

00:13:27.207 --> 00:13:29.090
- But I totally agree with you.

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:32.370
Like no one food in one dose
is going to cause disease.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.440
If you go, if you eat birthday cake,

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:36.430
if you eat a steak, you
know, once in a while,

00:13:36.430 --> 00:13:39.020
like no one food in one dose
is gonna cause a problem.

00:13:39.020 --> 00:13:42.520
- Do you think for a patient
who's diagnosed with obesity

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:43.840
or morbid obesity.

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:45.830
Do you think it's more
important for this patient

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:48.090
to look at their caloric intakes,

00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:50.160
or more important to look
at the types of foods

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:50.993
they're consuming?

00:13:50.993 --> 00:13:51.826
- I think it depends.

00:13:51.826 --> 00:13:54.970
So at my practice, we
have registered dieticians

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:57.160
and we have obesity medicine specialties.

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:59.490
So we do a multidisciplinary approach.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:02.450
Some patients who are obese
or have morbid obesity,

00:14:02.450 --> 00:14:04.610
some patients benefit from
gastric bypass surgery.

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:06.880
Like the best data we have is actually

00:14:06.880 --> 00:14:08.620
no specific dietary pattern

00:14:08.620 --> 00:14:10.570
is actually gastric bypass surgery,

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:12.020
the results are remarkable.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:14.580
And so I think a multi-pronged
approach is important.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:16.550
When it comes to the
dietary portion of it,

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:18.210
you know, if you look at the data

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:21.520
of what people can sustain,
we have research showing

00:14:21.520 --> 00:14:24.520
that people can stay
on keto for two years.

00:14:24.520 --> 00:14:25.353
We have research showing

00:14:25.353 --> 00:14:26.850
you can stay on plant-based for two years,

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:29.200
we have research showing
that you could do whatever.

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:30.970
I think it's most important to work

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:32.280
with a registered dietician

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:35.330
and find something that works for you.

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:38.510
If that's small changes
and sometimes people,

00:14:38.510 --> 00:14:40.350
I'm sure you see this
in your practice too.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:42.990
Sometimes patients can
do unbelievably well,

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:44.290
just cutting out soda.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:47.010
- Last point here, it's
something that you've mentioned

00:14:47.010 --> 00:14:48.190
before you struggled with.

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:51.740
There's an unfortunate
tie-in that has developed

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:53.440
in the "natural foods community"

00:14:53.440 --> 00:14:56.510
and has now reached into
the vegan community as well,

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.420
surrounding being anti-vaccine.

00:14:59.420 --> 00:15:01.160
Talk me through how that's come about

00:15:01.160 --> 00:15:02.880
and how do you speak to that subject?

00:15:02.880 --> 00:15:06.370
- The one thing that has unified
Nutrition Twitter this year

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:09.300
is that there's a huge
presence of anti-vax.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.080
And I think that all of
us who are physicians

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:14.570
in this space who are
not anti-vax like me,

00:15:14.570 --> 00:15:16.370
have kind of all bonded over this fact.

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:18.690
And I think it all comes
down to the same thing.

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:21.160
There's this inaccurate belief

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.640
that health is just a pull yourself up

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:26.700
by your bootstraps method and
that everyone should be able

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:28.270
to just fix their health immediately

00:15:28.270 --> 00:15:31.690
with their own willpower,
not respecting the fact that

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:33.970
each individual has certain circumstances

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:37.170
that maybe make it more
difficult for them to lose weight

00:15:37.170 --> 00:15:39.570
or follow a certain
dietary pattern, et cetera.

00:15:39.570 --> 00:15:41.760
And so part of this whole idea that people

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.480
in some of these diet groups have is that,

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.770
this pull yourself up by
your bootstraps mentality

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:49.630
is that well, COVID isn't a big deal

00:15:49.630 --> 00:15:51.710
and that we don't need vaccines.

00:15:51.710 --> 00:15:53.640
And then also in the
natural health community,

00:15:53.640 --> 00:15:56.770
there's obviously a ton of misinformation

00:15:56.770 --> 00:15:58.600
about what vaccines are,

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:01.970
what they do to you, that
they change your DNA.

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:05.870
And so to me, the most
important thing to emphasize

00:16:05.870 --> 00:16:07.700
to my patients, and I
do this with everything

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:10.930
from vaccines to statins
to diabetes medication,

00:16:10.930 --> 00:16:14.840
is that this binary idea
that it has to be lifestyle

00:16:14.840 --> 00:16:17.280
or medicine to me is
where we got it wrong.

00:16:17.280 --> 00:16:18.710
- As the expert I'd like to leave you

00:16:18.710 --> 00:16:20.700
with the final word here.

00:16:20.700 --> 00:16:23.790
Take us through what you
find to be the optimal diet

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:27.340
for us to follow, and
how should we be thinking

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:30.110
about nutrition and
health into the future?

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:31.400
- There is no perfect diet.

00:16:31.400 --> 00:16:33.830
Chasing the perfect diets is a fallacy.

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:37.030
I think that just focusing
on the overall context

00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:39.840
of your dietary pattern,
meaning eating more plants,

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:42.800
so more fruits, vegetables,
whole grains, legumes,

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:45.420
nuts and seeds, you
know, limiting red meat,

00:16:45.420 --> 00:16:48.350
processed foods, these
things can be beneficial.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:50.560
And just looking at the overall context

00:16:50.560 --> 00:16:51.740
of your dietary pattern,

00:16:51.740 --> 00:16:54.940
I think, that's the best that we can say

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:58.220
from the data we have
overall is most important.

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:00.180
- I actually went vegan for 30 days,

00:17:00.180 --> 00:17:01.370
and a whole bunch of other diets,

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:02.660
click here to check it out

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:04.542
and as always stay happy and healthy.

00:17:04.542 --> 00:17:07.542
(soft upbeat music)

